### What is this question really asking?

The interviewer is checking three things: whether your ambition is real, whether you are self-aware about the path to get there, and whether your goals fit what this company can offer. An answer that names a title the company does not have or reveals an exit plan ("I want to start my own company") signals a short-term hire.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class AmbitionCheck {
    enum Verdict { INVEST, SKIP }

    public static Verdict evaluate(boolean goalsAlignWithCompany, boolean selfAware) {
        return (goalsAlignWithCompany && selfAware) ? Verdict.INVEST : Verdict.SKIP;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, true));   // INVEST
        System.out.println(evaluate(false, true));  // SKIP
    }
}
```

```text
INVEST
SKIP
```

### How do you structure the answer around three growth axes?

The three axes give the answer substance without over-committing to a title. Technical depth: "I want to become the go-to person for payments and fraud architecture." Leadership: "I want to lead design reviews and mentor two or three junior engineers." Business impact: "I want to point to outcomes the bank cares about, like lower fraud losses."

```java
package com.bank.interview;

import java.util.List;

public class GrowthAxes {
    record Axis(String name, String fiveYearGoal) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Axis> axes = List.of(
            new Axis("Technical depth", "Go-to person for fraud architecture"),
            new Axis("Leadership",      "Lead design reviews, mentor 2-3 juniors"),
            new Axis("Business impact", "Measurably lower fraud losses")
        );
        axes.forEach(a -> System.out.println(a.name() + ": " + a.fiveYearGoal()));
    }
}
```

```text
Technical depth: Go-to person for fraud architecture
Leadership: Lead design reviews, mentor 2-3 juniors
Business impact: Measurably lower fraud losses
```

### Why should you talk about capability instead of a title?

Titles vary between companies. "Staff engineer" at one company is "principal" at another. If you name a title the company does not use, the interviewer is confused. If you describe the capability level, the interviewer maps it to their own ladder.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class CapabilityOverTitle {
    enum Level { ENGINEER, SENIOR, STAFF, PRINCIPAL }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Level current = Level.SENIOR;
        String trajectory = "Growing from " + current + " toward "
            + Level.STAFF + "-level ownership: platform design, tech direction, mentoring.";
        System.out.println(trajectory);
    }
}
```

```text
Growing from SENIOR toward STAFF-level ownership: platform design, tech direction, mentoring.
```

### What are common traps to avoid?

The first trap is naming a specific manager title, which can backfire if the team is flat or the role is purely technical. The second trap is saying "running my own startup" or "doing an MBA", which signals you plan to leave. The third trap is being too vague: "I just want to grow" gives the interviewer nothing.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

import java.util.Map;

public class FiveYearTraps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, String> traps = Map.of(
            "I want to be a VP",           "Trap: title the company may not have",
            "I plan to start my startup",  "Trap: signals you will leave",
            "I just want to grow",         "Trap: too vague to evaluate"
        );
        traps.forEach((answer, issue) -> System.out.println(answer + " -> " + issue));
    }
}
```

### How do you tie it back to this role?

End the answer by connecting the five-year vision to the role in front of you. "This role is exactly the kind of place where that growth happens, because it sits at the intersection of payments depth and system design scope." This makes the interviewer see hiring you as the first step in a multi-year investment.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class TieToRole {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String vision = "Technical authority in payments and fraud";
        String thisRole = "Payments backend with fraud-detection scope";
        System.out.println("5-year vision: " + vision);
        System.out.println("This role: " + thisRole);
        System.out.println("Fit: this role is step one on that path.");
    }
}
```

```text
5-year vision: Technical authority in payments and fraud
This role: Payments backend with fraud-detection scope
Fit: this role is step one on that path.
```

### How does the STAR lens apply to a future question?

Through STAR this is a forward-projected story. The Situation is where you are now in seniority. The Task is the capability gap between now and five years out. The Action is the deliberate learning and ownership you will pursue. The Result is the engineer you will have become.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class FutureSTAR {
    record STAR(String situation, String task, String action, String result) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        STAR fiveYear = new STAR(
            "Senior engineer owning one transfer service",
            "Gap: platform-level design and mentoring scope",
            "Lead design reviews, own fraud architecture, mentor juniors",
            "Staff-level authority in payments and fraud"
        );
        System.out.println(fiveYear);
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer emphasize about mentoring?

A big part of growing in seniority is multiplying your impact through other people rather than just writing more code yourself. Mention that in five years you want to be growing the next generation of engineers on the team.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class MentoringGoal {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int juniorsToMentor = 3;
        String method = "Weekly design reviews + pair programming on hard bugs";
        System.out.println("Mentoring " + juniorsToMentor + " engineers via: " + method);
        System.out.println("Impact: multiply my output through others.");
    }
}
```

```text
Mentoring 3 engineers via: Weekly design reviews + pair programming on hard bugs
Impact: multiply my output through others.
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

After this question, expect: "How do you plan to get there?" (describe specific learning), "What if that role does not exist here?" (focus on capabilities, not titles), and "Are you interested in management?" (answer honestly based on the company's ladder).

```java
package com.bank.interview;

import java.util.List;

public class FollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> followUps = List.of(
            "How do you plan to get there? -> Specific learning + ownership milestones",
            "What if that role does not exist? -> I focus on capabilities, not titles",
            "Interested in management? -> Answer honestly per their ladder"
        );
        followUps.forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```
